### Python Inspiration
<hr class="section-rule lvl-3">

Makefile is already a peculiar language because it handles 2 syntaxes simultaneously: one for shell, and one for Makefile-proper.  If you're using `compose.mk` for [polyglots]({{_}}/demos/polyglots) or [matrioshkas]({{_}}/demos/matrioshka) or [other supported idioms](#from-style-to-dialect), that can add even more syntax.  This can make readability challenging, but strong conventions can help a lot.

With `compose.mk`, much inspiration is taken from python.  A few random examples: 

* Default target is `__main__` unless `.DEFAULT_GOAL` is explicitly set
* Double-under "specials" like `__file__`, `__main__`, and `__doc__` are reserved for special roles (see the [dunder-name index]({{_}}/config#dunder-names))
* Usage of `self` conventionally indicates a non-public interface, e.g. with [compose.bind.target]({{_}}/container-dispatch) 
* Usage of make-prereqs as [pseudo-functions](#arguments-parameters-pipes) resembles decorators
* Python-style docstrings for targets [are supported]({{_}}/cli-help/#docstring-style)
* Python-style [indentation for recipe bodies]({{_}}/cmk/compiler/#indentation) (tabs *or* spaces; mismatches error like `IndentationError`)
* [Emulation of]({{_}}/signals/#at-exit-handlers) the `atexit` module semantics
* [Support for keyword-args]({{_}}/standard-lib#argument-parsing)

### General Naming Conventions
<hr class="section-rule lvl-3">

1. Dot-path style namespacing are a honking great idea (e.g. `module.target`).
1. Snake-case style target names are recommended when they will help with tab-completion (e.g. `module.target_name`).
1. Kebab-case for targets (e.g. `my-target-name`) is fairly common in Makefiles out in the wild, so it is **explicitly reserved for project code**, never used for targets in `compose.mk`, and is not recommended for library-style extensions of `compose.mk`.
1. Dot-paths should be used instead of snake-case if targets resemble keywords. i.e. if `_` is just too onerous and `.` is faster.  For example {{site.api_link('flux.if.then',mkdocs,arg='targets')}}.
1. Avoid custom functions in `make`, but if you need them, differentiate functions from vars using `_` as a prefix to indicate args are required.
1. Differentiate "public" targets (i.e. the CLI's API) from internal helpers by using a `self`-qualified prefix or simply a leading `.`

### Module Headers
<hr class="section-rule lvl-3">

A `.cmk` module or plugin opens with a small, predictable header: a one-line **executive summary**, then the `cmk_pragma` (if any), then a `USAGE:` line.  The pragma sits *immediately below the executive summary*, pretty-printed (spaced, not minified), and fenced by a blank comment line above and below.  Fuller detail, the tool list, config table, mechanics, and see-also, belongs in a `'''...'''` module docstring (which lowers to `__doc__`), not in the `#` header.

```Makefile {.snippet .language-makefile}
#!/usr/bin/env -S ./compose.mk cmk compile
#
# <name>.cmk: one-line executive summary.
#
# cmk_pragma ::: { "kind": "module" } :::
#
# USAGE: <how to load / run it>

'''
Fuller detail lives here: the tool list, config table, mechanics, see-also.
'''
```

Keep `$(call ...)` and `${...}` out of the docstring so `__doc__` stays inert; it is a deferred `define`, so a stray call or variable reference there would fire if the doc were ever expanded.  A `USAGE:` line that must show a `$(call ...)` keeps it up in the `#` header, where comment text is never expanded. <!-- docs-code-ok -->

### Bind Declarations
<hr class="section-rule lvl-3">

Bind declarations for targets are similar to function-decorators, and [actually look like decorators in cmk-lang]({{_}}/cmk/compiler#bind-declarations).  But what is being "bound" to what here?

Sometimes bind-declarations attach external variables as local defaults, allowing [targets to support keyword-args]({{_}}/standard-lib#argument-parsing)

!!! goals "<strong>Binding Variables and Scopes:</strong>"
	* [bind.posargs]({{_}}/standard-lib/#bind-from-parameters)
	* [bind.args(from=env)]({{_}}/standard-lib/#bind-from-environment)
	* [bind.args(from=json)]({{_}}/standard-lib/#bind-from-json)
	* [def.to.env]({{_}}/standard-lib/#bind-def-env)

Sometimes bind-declarations bind existing code(s) or targets, to an existing runtime context like a target or a container.

!!! goals "<strong>Binding Tasks and Runtimes:</strong>"
	* [compose.bind.target]({{_}}/container-dispatch/#container-agnostic-dispatch)
	* [compose.bind.script]({{_}}/container-dispatch/#container-agnostic-dispatch)
	* [docker.bind.script]({{_}}/raw-docker/#dispatch-scripts-in-containers)
	* [mk.docker.bind.script]({{_}}/raw-docker/#dispatch-scripts-in-containers)

Bind-declarations like in the 2nd category usually have an import-equivalent.  You can think of these as *import partials*, i.e. you bring your own target for the sake of being explicit, whereas with an [import statement](#import-statement) the target name would usually be implied.

### Import Statements
<hr class="section-rule lvl-3">

Whereas bind-declarations attach things, import-statements create (or "scaffold") new targets dynamically.  All of this is documented elsewhere, but this section puts the related stuff in one place.

!!! goals "<strong>Targets from Docker Images or Dockerfiles:</strong>"
